/* book exam traps: Capacitance is not proportional to tank volume in an irregular tank; it follows the wetted height and area between the plates. | Empty probe capacitance is not zero. Air gives about 35 pF in the example; zero would mean a short or an open circuit. | Do not mix fuel flow (kg/h) with fuel quantity (kg). The gauge shows total; flow must be integrated over time. | The dielectric constant of fuel is not fixed. Temperature and fuel type shift it, and the compensator exists for that; density also shifts. | A quantity gauge can be correct in litres and wrong in kilograms if density is wrong; read the units. | Reading a torque gauge as power. Power is torque times shaft speed, so the same torque at a lower propeller speed is less power. */
